th only 75 percent there are  a small number of  posi positive specimens in this mix um  the the n1  n2 cycle thresholds in the cd  cdc test uh were for the false negatives  are listed here  and they're in the 30s uh low 30s to  high 30s  i'm not sure what to to make of that the  referral laboratory also reported three  inconclusive specimens  that they were unable to reproduce the  results  they tested negative by abbott so faced  with this  we wanted to do a better job of looking  at the how well the abbott  assay compared to the cdc eu assay so  the 12 specimens that we received from  our local public health lab they were  randomly selected  and they the positive and negative  predictive values were  100  we did so looking at the  i'm sorry the the second bullet point or  the locally performed  test uh the first bullet point or what  we got from the public health lab in  both cases the positive predictive  agreement was a hundred percent  so overall if you put all three data  sets together we found a 91 percent  uh positive predictive agreement and 100  negative predictive agreement between  the results of the cdc and the abbott  test  and but we did see there with the  positive predictive agreement was  dependent uh upon where the test was  performed so  there is some interlaboratory variation  in in that  in terms of how the results  [Music]  compare to the eu to the cdc  eua assay  um the clinical verification also  included a comparison  uh to the the  respiratory virus panel results from  biofire  uh there were 168 specimens that were  concordant  negative for for covid uh um for sars  coronavirus 2 rna  we found other respiratory pathogens in  71 of those or 42 percent  and i'm not going to read all of the  different viruses we recovered but the  important thing is highlighted there  they were in this data set there were 18  uh specimens that were positive for  seasonal coronaviruses  uh which was actually um quite  interesting because that's more  um posit