ou go from 2  4 8 16 32  64 etc so you can approximate the viral  load  so we know that for example that  100 is our lod so you can use this  sort of simple formula 100 times 2  raised to the limit of the ct at the  limit of detection  minus the ct for any given assay  to come up with an approximate borrower  load so  and that sort of makes sense because if  your ct was 25  that means you're basically one  your resulting one  cycle earlier so you would expect that  your viral load would be twice as high  as your limited detection  if you and if you plug that into the  formula you get a viral load of 200  if you're one cycle later then you're  limited detection  you plug into the formula you get a bar  load of 50.  well of course nothing in life is that  simple because  amplification efficiency is less than  100  and it decreases with cycle number  and you could these are  graphs of two  positive samples one with a very low ct  and one on the right with a very high  ct near the limit of detection and you  can see that there's  more sort of the slope is steeper  with a sample with a very high viral  load that gets detected earlier  and as you go through additional  amplification  signals to reach your threshold the  amplification becomes less efficient  by basically looking at  a lot of different curves like this  we're able to empirically establish a  formula  which we think fairly accurately  predicts the viral load  and from this from the ct value and  we've  sort of published  some of this data now in bio  archive if you're interested in more  detail so  i'll just leave it that we think that we  have a pretty good approximation of  viral load  and so then we looked at  our uh data from march 26 to may  2nd and you know that was sort of our  big surge  in the state of massachusetts so we did  27  000 tests we had 6 000 positives  4 700 unique patients and so we had two  questions  so for a viral load to mean something  one of the questions was well is it  repeatable  if you swab someone's nose and  you get in twice in a row and you've got  a bar load of  a hundred one time and a hundred billion  a second  then fire loads would not be clinically  useful because we wouldn't really they  wouldn't necessarily mean anything  and the second question was can we learn  anything from the viral load  distribution  and would that help us understand how to  use our testing  so in fact the ct values and therefore  the viral loads  are reproducible between nasopharyngeal  swabs collected across  short time intervals so in our very  large data set we had a bunch of  patients  who had repeat collections either within  six hours or within 12 hours of each  other we don't know the reasons why  and then we plotted the ct value from  the first collection  versus the ct value of the second  collection you can see  that we have a nice sort of linear  relationship throughout the range of ct  values that we  um would obtain so it looks uh like  um uh those ct values are  repeatable in a clinical sense if you  sample patients  uh more than once so they're telling us  truly  about a status  of our patients  okay so then we um  decided to basically we converted our ct  values  for patient first positives and  we that's about 4 700 patients and  we basically binned those  those viral load results and  to show the fraction of cases in each  one half log 10 bin and so  our viral loads range from very low up  to a billion  copies genome copies per ml and one of  the fascinating things was  that the distribution was fairly even  and so you see basically about the same  number a fraction of cases are  distributed  throughout the range of detection except  for  you know a drop off below 100 copies per  ml which is the limit of detection so  we um considered that that was  reflective  of the limit of detection of the assay  rather than  an actual uh change in distribution  so why this occurred occurs biologically  is not clear and perhaps as hand-waving  but  