ect with  the  sort of best-in-class uh pcr  methods one of the things that i should  mention and one of the problems in the  field and again this is  early and we're in a crisis situation is  there's no standard  uh there's sort of no reference standard  and  a lot of these for benchmarking your  assays so the limit of detection  um and these uh uh a reported limit of  detections have been  um uh uh determined using different  reference materials  so um you know i i would say that  you know take this graph as an  approximation  and there's clearly a case for some  universal standard  for benchmarking so that we as a  clinical community  can really understand the differences  between these assays  okay when all this happened it was a  chance to review sort of the anatomy of  the nasal passages  we have nasopharyngeal swabs which  proceed backwards into the you know  basically all the way back  um uh to the uh  nasopharynx and in red um we have nasal  swabs that  uh that sample more interiorly  and importantly the enter nasal passages  uh  are lined with squamous epithelium the  virus  replicates in ciliated respiratory  epithelium so there's  some theoretical concern that anterior  nasal swabs may not necessarily  sample the right place so we know that  nasopharyngeal swabs appear to have the  best  sensitivity and they clearly sample  respiratory  epithelium however they're difficult to  collect you need  uh trained collection staff and they're  uncomfortable we don't have people that  want to come back and volunteer  for repeat testing unless under duress  nasal swabs are easy to collect however  they may not fully sample the  respiratory epithelium  depending upon their depth and  there's difference in the literature  regarding performance of nasal swabs  so we  performed a multi-arm  clinical trial to compare the  performance of nasopharyngeal swabs  to nasal swabs in our patient population  and we basically shown in this graph  or the ct values with the nasal s